[dh07+@andrew.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:]

>you should NEVER get a named weapon from polymorph. polymorphing stashes
>is a (too) powerful technique and sometimes you'll only get one maybe two
>wishes in a game so ...

From a playability standpoint you are absolutely right - it would be boring
to pick up every last piece of orc junk and drag it around to a stash in 
the hopes of picking up a polymorph wand - which is exactly why I didn't
wish for a wand of polymorph when I got lucky with the water demon. I chose
to ignore the (mis?)feature.  (Besides, I *LOVE* speed boots.)

>the wizard's athame is also handy ...

I didn't know this.  Please elaborate (or hint)

>>  7) That tinning kit is very useful! ...
>the problem with tins is that they're not very nutritious on average,
>otherwise tinning is excellent technique.

Agreed.  The main point was the tinning of corpses (such as wraiths)
that were inconvenient to eat at the moment, and the tinning of dead
trolls to prevent them from rising from the dead.  Since then, I have
been told (unconfirmed) that eating a troll gives automatic regeneration 
for a certain period of time.  If so, then those tins of troll meat
could be really useful.

>rust-proof your armor (you know how) and then get rid of all Liches. i
>like to get rid of the cockatrice and the golden naga.

Genociding the cockatrice was automatic for me in earlier nethack versions,
but now carrying a few lizard corpses seems to suffice.  Liches presented
no real problems with the speed boots on (actually no monster really did).
I don't recall tangling with a golden naga.

Rustproofing armor requires several scrolls, one for the helmet, one for
the boots (if metal), and one for the gauntlets of power (if I have em).
Of course I usually have a cloak. (Am I missing something?)
Since I normally discover the scroll of genocide by reading an unblessed
one, the rust monster seems to be the best choice -- maybe a golden naga
will have the pleasure of changing my mind.

OK Dave, how about this: A new shop (rare), the "Ziebart" store.  You
walk in, drop a piece of armor or a weapon, and the shopkeeper offers
to rustproof it for you (for a fee).

>well, i hope that you don't mind that i've interjected my opinions.

